#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124246 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "e5f16c73-7422-57db-9f63-d1e593b8c3be" :question/math-academy-id "e-5442" :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{b}.$ In the affirmative case, find such a vector.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/36675dd67326016b3083bfb87ec93ae427a74879ce41007a4cadf11f6cfca80d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $\\mathbf{T},$ we have $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we need to check if the equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10 & 26 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has a zero row, and so the system is consistent. We have two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-8x_2-16x_3 = 8\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_2=-1-2x_3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n2x_1+4(-1-2x_3)+2x_3 = 6\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=5+3x_3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/72aba565539bdf6cee541e7495665a5bd422f8e7c5693ab68794c0a284c42e8d.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=0,$ we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/9322232405d24c433a3870b3a29d8deb8c85e01e5a88eabcc48e51be75c21e9d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124247 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "5b6f9536-7ac0-57d6-b5cd-d9ce4f4e2620" :question/math-academy-id "e-5443" :question/problem "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/71adf512017629aa4cfc010c18bc840ea63459818bae4ebc0b7dba59193c0d00.png) is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ then what is $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T})?$" :question/worked-solution "In general, $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{7}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{rank}(T)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124274 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "372bf9eb-6665-5593-8aab-0fa31f5483d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5619" :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/1c8969401a7c812e945fdc0b2f1774f6cd1d1d9c35269b2b20ea8165449d427f.png) be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/05f7384dd957b2616433104bfefbb8b3e7aaa619dbb1dff94fec43c8a99e6c3c.png) belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ if there exists a vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/be82bf02cdfe6a50557b312ba78708463248f844f81016df2cda41c6cfbb6c1a.png) such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ -x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y=4 \\\\ -x+y=k \\\\ 2y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $y=4,$ which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+4 & =4 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+y & =k \\\\ 0+4 & =k \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ when $k=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43fb1cff-eb7b-5c4f-9d59-b504c5815c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370694} {:answer/id #uuid "411e8276-5be0-566c-a3b0-9bfe37c293fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370695} {:answer/id #uuid "27960ac7-ce8b-52cb-9cdb-149f72feaac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370696} {:answer/id #uuid "84498a9d-37d3-50d1-b7a8-0189f044813e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370697} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2ebae7-85ce-5d13-a97d-aebd16adf8b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84498a9d-37d3-50d1-b7a8-0189f044813e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370697} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f551e2-b5bd-5f0b-89a6-edf6c0b170f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3075d573-ecbb-5abb-8df9-7d90dddcc207" :question/math-academy-id "q-140068" :question/problem "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$  belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2x-4y \\\\ x-3y \\\\ -x+4y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y=2 \\\\ x-3y=7 \\\\ -x+4y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=7+3y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y & =2 \\\\ 2(7+3y)-4y & =2 \\\\ 2y & =-12 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7+3y \\\\  & =7+3(-6) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-11$ and $y=-6$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =k \\\\ -(-11)+4(-6) & =k \\\\ k & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7e456be-b9a8-5b48-92ad-867b0456ccf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370701} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8db685-c2b4-5905-974e-8f7687c4ab3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370702} {:answer/id #uuid "87de86ea-7da5-5cd5-8042-014c717edbcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370703} {:answer/id #uuid "93d650e3-b3ed-516b-b4a9-d1998fb750f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370704} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f47fc5-e63c-5ec1-8704-0be6d25aebd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7e456be-b9a8-5b48-92ad-867b0456ccf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370701} :answer-field/id #uuid "e14d54b2-1932-567c-beb1-1c63f9028caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6eb79221-545d-59dc-9b3c-bfa872a652f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-140075" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ -11{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417cde11-649f-57fa-a300-8e3ed8c046a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370708} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2d86b-4a25-5c3b-a9ee-256aa2d5ee57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370709} {:answer/id #uuid "a69d9aa1-6f47-5793-a69d-21c34ea6b229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370710} {:answer/id #uuid "5e607a4b-cf86-543c-a1e9-d3c8b8d9e1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370711} {:answer/id #uuid "5e667ad6-5191-53a7-b4c0-989a0a597353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71c2d86b-4a25-5c3b-a9ee-256aa2d5ee57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370709} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ea623b6-f081-58d6-a380-b170ebaef987" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f84d3cc4-6812-54c8-824c-60f072940eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-37937" :question/problem "If $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{14}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e455955-0a6a-59ff-822c-d50c52f1b5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370715} {:answer/id #uuid "f24256f0-27d6-5a77-b9ab-281aab4e06c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370716} {:answer/id #uuid "e1aa100c-4f0c-58d0-a1b0-61f9b115b7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370717} {:answer/id #uuid "a13f1ce9-49d5-5d02-9187-d7bf20b08f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370718} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b47fbb-8cd7-5a5b-ba5f-0f4510b168b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e455955-0a6a-59ff-822c-d50c52f1b5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370715} :answer-field/id #uuid "26baa776-c997-5821-a7ee-616fe1fa9096" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94a593ef-f427-52ab-b631-04b2340b544d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37939" :question/problem "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y+z \\\\ z+y-x \\\\ z-2y\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." :question/worked-solution "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "807456cf-78c0-544f-bc96-667eff400128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370721} {:answer/id #uuid "acde5cbb-b147-5745-a71d-6f105da2d472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370722} {:answer/id #uuid "94eb96f0-efb0-5a7a-803e-77df7eb70b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370723} {:answer/id #uuid "7be4ee43-5272-5ea2-ab2d-5018cc3c7dfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370724} {:answer/id #uuid "a4651318-7d46-588d-8642-b449779c192a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7be4ee43-5272-5ea2-ab2d-5018cc3c7dfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370724} :answer-field/id #uuid "0072e1ae-1d62-5800-a7fe-73bbd8e88fc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bed1a457-fd38-5a3f-94a3-0c655ad8bedf" :question/math-academy-id "q-38001" :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{11}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-11{x}_{2}+11{x}_{3}=11\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}-5(-1+{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2-{x}_{3} \\\\ -1+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2-1=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d36825-81b7-5a43-9043-a6472f4c0e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370728} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb39b17-d028-5313-baf4-83d0d32f78b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370729} {:answer/id #uuid "3074fc29-497b-5d73-9a49-94a669b72c00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370730} {:answer/id #uuid "25d74e11-0962-5547-94b8-ccaa81a39d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370731} {:answer/id #uuid "ad33cb2e-861e-5e95-b365-29742bc0f8f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34d36825-81b7-5a43-9043-a6472f4c0e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370728} :answer-field/id #uuid "05600aef-6f68-587d-b643-abc3a7b4d92d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15e27970-4acc-5263-a8ac-f25941fc07a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38006" :question/problem "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}w-x+2y-z \\\\ x-y-z \\\\ 4w-y+z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." :question/worked-solution "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034f9c56-c97e-5156-8327-fff0afefef76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370734} {:answer/id #uuid "b90fefbb-8f33-5845-b764-b4fef4fec9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370735} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1bf4c1-fbc8-5b60-a01a-3aa64edbe9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370736} {:answer/id #uuid "167be7c9-ac3c-59e7-9742-026e45679fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370737} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5bbb48-915e-5ee8-a485-d8e1028bdc0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b90fefbb-8f33-5845-b764-b4fef4fec9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370735} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b654332-f144-5b14-b302-11d7550290ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "276425ed-7889-5b58-948f-0c76f30f34c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38007" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87b1fbbb-72df-5a45-a327-d7b2776a02ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123311} {:answer/id #uuid "88753680-8d6f-58a3-8905-e3b2cb1e2962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123312} {:answer/id #uuid "96ef4f05-ac30-57ac-ae92-88cf2018b893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123313} {:answer/id #uuid "36aece73-192c-5554-8400-34a4ff1294d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123314} {:answer/id #uuid "36bb3421-743b-5ce1-b05e-e33fbf4b93c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36aece73-192c-5554-8400-34a4ff1294d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123314} :answer-field/id #uuid "74075aa9-9daf-5f9a-8893-27e2c204f26f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aff78f44-088e-5944-87f1-2279a4c5e2ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-38615" :question/problem "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=6 \\\\ -x+4y=-4 \\\\ -3x-5y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ -2(4+4y)+y & =6 \\\\ -7y & =14 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-2) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-5y & =k \\\\ -3(-4)-5(-2) & =k \\\\ k & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=22.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc19eb5-a401-5d49-94a7-2620dc93bad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370741} {:answer/id #uuid "44bfa3ee-d78d-5989-8991-1de43f4ef4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370742} {:answer/id #uuid "18f012cc-4002-5c06-a4cf-9102c0520e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370743} {:answer/id #uuid "f6209ca8-59d0-54cc-af62-46049f9fcc83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370744} {:answer/id #uuid "da5b25eb-9b86-5666-982b-91c061e83587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bc19eb5-a401-5d49-94a7-2620dc93bad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370741} :answer-field/id #uuid "11ecd6de-333e-5fe8-92db-d0696fbb3ee5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2dc778fa-5a47-56a3-8dad-82a1ece20129" :question/math-academy-id "q-38625" :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -28 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 & -28 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 4 & 26\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8 & 16\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=-8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-4+2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-5-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -4+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7+(-2)=-9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d93e3e-4780-5438-b40b-6528b4f4adbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123317} {:answer/id #uuid "d1219f5c-5aa8-54b4-a6c2-19c3a1736495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123318} {:answer/id #uuid "11e68785-5cc8-5e32-bac3-c70f63042ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123319} {:answer/id #uuid "600109c1-87f3-5d3b-86ca-ae878048a5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123320} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5fe583-a72f-5724-b1cd-3b73a0043db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e68785-5cc8-5e32-bac3-c70f63042ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123319} :answer-field/id #uuid "612cfb5a-c629-5363-a78c-971d8dbcfa35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5471fadb-1b86-5cb7-bba6-ebb075794d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-38627" :question/problem "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 2x-4y \\\\ -2x+10y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-8y=6 \\\\ 2x-4y=8 \\\\ -2x+10y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-8y & =6 \\\\ 3(4+2y)-8y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =-6 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+2y \\\\  & =4+2(3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+10y & =k \\\\ -2(10)+10(3) & =k \\\\ k & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a371f3-585e-5a64-8db6-93bc48e17917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370748} {:answer/id #uuid "24678ecd-cbb1-5347-a104-c215f159e6b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370749} {:answer/id #uuid "10efd3d8-21ae-5586-a0c3-7ab7d7321f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370750} {:answer/id #uuid "093f245f-8511-5ed6-81a6-d0723942a6e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370751} {:answer/id #uuid "8051f3dd-927e-51eb-83d6-eb71bd11b839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10efd3d8-21ae-5586-a0c3-7ab7d7321f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370750} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0841031-ae47-51e9-8099-4c85fbfab868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "752e8b62-e28e-532d-b1d4-07d2ef080868" :question/math-academy-id "q-38629" :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $w-x+y,$ where $w,x,y,z$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}w-3x-2z \\\\ 2x-2w-4y \\\\ 3w-y-10x-7z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -10 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -10 & -1 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-8.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90b9f09e-06eb-597a-8e8e-6809d6c36e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123419} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fdeef1-b290-55c5-8869-0d9c213b5467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123420} {:answer/id #uuid "f7187eae-6cd3-5033-8042-7ab63c2235d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123421} {:answer/id #uuid "335d6e5c-d705-5d76-9064-541f823f99d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123422} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5a75c4-6d4e-54c4-8c19-f7515d73fe9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7fdeef1-b290-55c5-8869-0d9c213b5467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123420} :answer-field/id #uuid "37a7684d-9d1f-500d-8b3d-16f48a3f445c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f485de0-d115-59d2-87d8-fc0e53ab516c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40210" :question/problem "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+2y \\\\ y-x+6z \\\\ 8z-x+3y\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." :question/worked-solution "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{5}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9098ea91-5360-57e6-952c-a56b5c8e1ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370755} {:answer/id #uuid "abc8c8a3-3a3b-55ce-935b-0976140c9367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370756} {:answer/id #uuid "0043ad67-f18c-5632-a885-d8099c720381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370757} {:answer/id #uuid "11d957b7-1d3a-5f3f-87c1-46ebff0d3742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370758} {:answer/id #uuid "816a9cf8-0592-525d-ada4-69b14aa7215e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11d957b7-1d3a-5f3f-87c1-46ebff0d3742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370758} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78ecc08-fdf3-538b-a844-8d9eb9c91873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14f2a862-261e-505b-887b-84469076bcb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80818" :question/problem "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ x+2y \\\\ -2x+7y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ x+2y \\\\ -2x+7y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=9 \\\\ x+2y=8 \\\\ -2x+7y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=8-2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =9 \\\\ 3(8-2y)+y & =9 \\\\ -5y & =-15 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =8-2y \\\\  & =8-2(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y & =k \\\\ -2(2)+7(3) & =k \\\\ k & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=17.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325b2fb3-3c98-5b24-8216-c7c0120f66ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370762} {:answer/id #uuid "134e4855-d6e7-5d4f-abed-d6e3c126f686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370763} {:answer/id #uuid "bb366bd3-d345-5a04-adc3-01eff45a854c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370764} {:answer/id #uuid "82adc085-dfd1-513d-b95b-a41696e644b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370765} {:answer/id #uuid "283e9c88-1f0e-587f-bef1-72501798b7f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "325b2fb3-3c98-5b24-8216-c7c0120f66ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370762} :answer-field/id #uuid "615861da-df52-58f4-98fd-db280534cdb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f3c56a7-03f9-59f7-8743-e0418935c95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81237" :question/problem "If $T=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 9 & -8 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 9 & -8 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac0f9947-1075-53d6-a44a-d59c9e95d366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370769} {:answer/id #uuid "cdabf739-1933-5c75-a758-d0d265efba24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370770} {:answer/id #uuid "e808a98e-ba84-5cb0-90a7-3f160b900cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370771} {:answer/id #uuid "a7294150-1549-5f99-9c88-1dc03da40d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370772} {:answer/id #uuid "fb481e11-aa2f-58bc-ae5b-e9223446fbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac0f9947-1075-53d6-a44a-d59c9e95d366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370769} :answer-field/id #uuid "2651c3d6-b74f-5ce6-b6b1-019dd9c3f8c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a5dfc69-ed48-51c7-a9d5-dee61f0e3afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-81242" :question/problem "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3x+2y \\\\ -2y \\\\ -5x+y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+2y=5 \\\\ -2y=k \\\\ -5x+y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $y=5x-1$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2y & =5 \\\\ -3x+2(5x-1) & =5 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x-1 \\\\  & =5(1)-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=4$ into the second equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y & =k \\\\ -2(4) & =k \\\\ k & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-8.$"}]}
